// Test simfile with hand-worked repeated arrows, jumps and streams, for calibrating
// ddr-trainer's chart analyzer (src/chart_analyzer.rs), whose tests check the values below.
// Generated by test_resources/make_test_simfiles.py, so edit that instead of this.
//
// One BPM (120, so quarter notes are 0.5 s apart, 8ths 0.25 s and 16ths 0.125 s), no stops.
//
// Techniques are looked for inside a segment: a run of notes with no gap longer than 2 s
// between two of them. Each one found has a speed in notes per second. For what a single
// note does, that is 1 over the gap since the note before: 8 for 16ths here, 4 for 8ths, 2
// for quarter notes, and 0 for a segment's first note. A technique's statistics are how many
// were found and the mean, median and max of their speeds, or 0 with none.
//   - a repeated arrow is a note that steps on an arrow the note right before it also stepped
//     on (hold heads count). So a run of 4 notes on one arrow is 3, a jump on the same two
//     arrows as the jump before it is 1, and quarter notes on one arrow are slow ones
//   - a jump is a note with 2 or more arrows
//   - a stream is 8 or more notes in a row at an even spacing (the longest gap at most 10%
//     over the shortest), as long as it goes. Its speed is its notes less one, over its
//     seconds. A change of rhythm ends it, and the next may start on its last note
//
//   chart      repeated arrows  speed: mean  median  max    jumps  speed: mean  median  max
//   Beginner    0               0            0       0      0      0            0       0
//   Easy       10               91/15        8       8      2      6            6       8
//   Medium      0               0            0       0      0      0            0       0
//   Hard       10               6.8          8       8      4      5            4       8
//   Challenge   9               296/81       2       8      4      85/18        5       8
//
//   chart      streams  notes  length: mean  median  max    speed: mean  median  max
//   Beginner   0        0      0             0       0      0            0       0
//   Easy       0        0      0             0       0      0            0       0
//   Medium     2        24     12            12      16     6            6       8
//   Hard       1        17     17            17      17     8            8       8
//   Challenge  1        16     16            16      16     4            4       4
//
// Each chart's repeated arrows, by time in seconds, with the gap before them in brackets:
//   Easy       0.25 0.75 (0.25)  1.5 (0.5)  2.125 2.25 2.5 2.625 2.75 (0.125)  3.125 (0.375)
//              3.25 (0.125): speeds 4 4 2 8 8 8 8 8 8/3 8
//   Hard       0.125 0.25 ... 0.875 (0.125)  2.25 2.5 2.75 (0.25): speeds 8 x 7, 4 x 3
//   Challenge  0.5 1 1.5 2 (0.5)  2.75 4.75 (0.25)  6.375 6.625 (0.125)  8 (1.125):
//              speeds 2 2 2 2 4 4 8 8 8/9
// Jumps: Easy at 2 (0.25) and 2.125 (0.125). Hard at 2 (0.125) and 2.25 2.5 2.75 (0.25).
//   Challenge at 6.25 (0.5), 6.375 and 6.5 (0.125), and 8 (1.125): speeds 2 8 8 8/9.
// Streams: Easy's longest even runs are 5 8ths and 7 16ths. Medium: the 8 8ths from 2 s, and
//   16 16ths from 4.125 s to 6 s (the first 8th of the next measure is 0.125 s after the
//   last 16th). Hard: 17 16ths from 0 s to 2 s. Challenge: 16 8ths from 2 s to 5.75 s; the
//   5 quarter notes before them and the 6 16ths after are too few.
#TITLE:Pattern Stats;
#ARTIST:ddr-trainer;
#MUSIC:pattern_stats.ogg;
#OFFSET:0.000000;
#BPMS:0.000=120.000;
#STOPS:;

